Student Justice Collective  - Northshore School District
Student Justice Collective
The Northshore School District invites high school students interested in serving on its Student Justice Collective to apply. The Racial and Educational Justice Department oversees the Collective.
Northshore is committed to creating a safe, caring, affirming, and inclusive environment within our school district community so that each and every student, family and staff member feels welcomed, valued, included, and supported. We recognize and advocate for the intersectional diversity in our district, schools, and community in relation to race, ethnicity, creed, religion, color, national origin, honorably discharged veteran or military status, sexual orientation, gender expression, gender identity, homelessness, immigration and citizenship status, the presence of any sensory, mental, or physical disabilities, neurodivergence, and the use of a trained dog guide or service animal by a person with a disability. Therefore, we have a responsibility to commit time, energy and resources to develop an equitable and inclusive environment for all students.
Purpose of the Student Justice Collective
Central Focuses of the Student Justice Collective
Role of the Student Justice Collective
Membership
The Racial and Educational Justice Department believes it is important to expand opportunities for youth leadership and agency, help students come together across schools, and co-construct a space in which students can learn with and from one another.
The Racial and Educational Justice Department and returning members of the Student Justice Collective have outlined a scope and sequence for this year's work. Knowing this space is student-led, new and returning members will co-design initiatives and action plans with support from the Racial and Educational Justice Department throughout the year. We will learn with and from each other about youth-led leadership, movements, and organizing strategies over time; build workshops for the 2025 Student Justice Conference; and develop various justice-driven initiatives.
Northshore School District’s Student Justice Collective serves as an advisory to the Racial and Educational Justice Department and advocates for our community at large. The Collective’s aim is to build community, organize in solidarity, and (re)imagine what a justice-driven education can and should look like. Northshore School District students from all of our high schools are encouraged to apply.
Members should be committed to serving:
At least a 1-year term
At a consistent attendance rate
We recognize that continued learning and community building will happen during students’ time in this Collective. We also believe it is imperative to be respectful of and about the diverse communities that are represented in the Collective and that we serve. To that end, part of what will help build and shape this youth community is restorative practice. Restorative circles will be utilized as a means to both 1) regularly build and shape community (ie: dialogue circles, celebration circles, healing/support circles) and 2) repair any harm that may have been done in/to our community space (ie: peace circles).
Student Justice Collective meetings are held two Wednesdays per month from 2:30 p.m. - 3:30 p.m.
