FAQ: Bond & Levies - Northshore School District
FAQ: Bond & Levies
If the state provides funding for K-12 education, why are local taxes needed?
What percentage of the vote is required for these measures to pass?
What is the validation requirement for school elections?
What is the anticipated tax impact for all measures?
Are the measures on the Feb. 10 ballot new taxes or will they replace expiring taxes?
How do the increasing home prices in our community affect what taxpayers will pay with these three measures?
Where can I get information about property tax exemptions for senior citizens or disabled persons?
Funding for education in the state of Washington is complicated. The state does provide some funding to school districts, but Northshore relies on voter-approved funding measures to bridge the gap between what the state funds and what our district needs to support students with programs, services, technology, and facilities. The state does not provide any funding for major construction projects in schools.
In order to fill these funding gap​s, school districts often utilize bonds and levies. This local funding allows our school district to provide the structures and services our community relies on, which allows students to grow and thrive.
Learn more about
school bond and levy funding in Washington state
.
In the Feb. 10 election, Northshore’s EP&O and Technology levies require a simple majority, or 50% + 1 of the votes cast, to pass. The Capital Projects Bond requires a super majority, or 60%, to pass.
The bond measure must meet a validation requirement, in which the number of ballots cast must total 40% or more of the total number of ballots cast in the previous General Election, in this case the November 2025 election. There is no validation requirement for levies.
The total combined tax rate of the February 2026 measures is estimated to be $3.20 per thousand of assessed property value. For a home valued at $1M, that would total $3,200 annually or $267 a month. This tax rate is actually lower than what our community approved in February 2022, which had a projected tax rate of $3.28.
